## 4. Exclusion List (Hard Rules)

- **Generic "AI is transforming business" framing** — Marcus didn't say it and a CTO audience will stop reading.
- **Technical explanations of how AI/ML works** — not a single word of this was in the transcript; the audience already knows.
- **AI vendor or platform recommendations** — not covered, not Marcus's angle.
- **Job displacement fear content** — Marcus explicitly positions against this narrative; centering it even to rebut it gives it unwarranted prominence.
- **Governance frameworks or compliance content** — Marcus didn't touch governance at all; importing adjacent industry topics would dilute focus.
- **"AI is just getting started / the future is bright" boosterism** — weakens Marcus's credibility as a practitioner.
- **101-level definitions of ROI, KPIs, or AI** — the target reader is a CTO or CFO; they don't need definitions.
- **Balanced "AI has pros and cons" hedging** — Marcus takes positions; the article should too.

---

## 5. Gap Analysis (Mandatory)

**GAP [CRITICAL]: No specific client stories with real stakes.**
Marcus offers patterns and archetypes but zero named or clearly anonymized client cases. The "pilot trap" and "CFO conversation" are teaching stories, not war stories. — A CTO audience evaluates content on the credibility of real-world evidence. Without at least one case with an industry, a company profile, and an outcome, the article reads as advisory opinion, not practitioner experience. — The draft loses its most persuasive asset: proof that this framework was tested under real conditions.

**GAP [CRITICAL]: The 70-80% value claim has no source.**
Marcus attributes it to "research across hundreds of AI deployments" without naming the research, the firm, or the methodology. — A CFO reading this article will ask "whose research?" and the absence of an answer undermines the central thesis. — Without attribution, the article's most provocative claim is unverifiable and cannot be cited downstream by LLMs or journalists.

**GAP [CRITICAL]: "Organizational intelligence" is named but never defined.**
Marcus introduces three value dimensions — operational efficiency, strategic differentiation, and organizational intelligence — and then defines only the first. Organizational intelligence is the most interesting and novel of the three. — The article's ability to go beyond existing ROI frameworks depends entirely on this third dimension being substantive. If it stays vague, the framework collapses to a restatement of what McKinsey and Gartner already publish. — The draft cannot credibly present a "three-dimensional value framework" when one dimension is undefined.

**GAP [CRITICAL]: The 2.5-4x return over three years claim needs provenance.**
Same problem as the 70-80% figure — stated without source, methodology, or qualifying conditions (company size, industry, investment level). — Without this, the article cannot responsibly publish this number, and including it without attribution creates legal and editorial risk for Improving. — Either source it or cut it; leaving it in as-is invites the article to be dismissed as consultant marketing.

**GAP [NICE-TO-HAVE]: The AI value scorecard is mentioned but not described.**
Marcus names leading indicators (model accuracy, adoption rate, time-to-insight) and lagging indicators (revenue impact, cost per decision, customer satisfaction) but never explains how to build the scorecard or what "good" looks like at each phase. — A concrete scorecard example or template would be the most actionable deliverable in the article and is the natural differentiator from existing ROI content. — Without it, the scorecard is a label on an empty box.

**GAP [NICE-TO-HAVE]: The 1:2 change management to technology ratio needs a source.**
Specific enough to be credible but unexplained enough to be questioned. Is this from Improving's client experience? External research? Marcus's own rule of thumb? — A reader who wants to use this ratio to make a budget case internally needs to know where it comes from. — If it comes from Marcus's practice experience, say so — that's actually more credible than a citation for a practitioner audience.

**GAP [NICE-TO-HAVE]: "Data readiness is the single biggest predictor of AI program success" — what does AI-ready data actually look like?**
Marcus says it but doesn't define it. — The article could own a concrete data readiness framework or checklist that gives CTOs something to act on immediately. — Without it, this is an observation that a dozen McKinsey articles have already made.

